# Post-Deploy FLS Prompt + Conditional FlexiPage (MANDATORY)

After EVERY successful, verified deploy of an object and/or its fields (single
object AND every object in a multi-object deploy), you MUST run BOTH gates below,
every time. They run AFTER `verify_deploy.py` confirms the fields landed and
AFTER the object's report tab is refreshed. Neither gate is skippable.

These gates layer on top of `sf-object-existence-precheck.mdc`,
`sf-deploy-delta-and-blockers.mdc`, and `sf-object-tab-and-app.mdc`.

## GATE 0 — Refresh the per-field Deployment-Status column (AI)

After verification, refresh column **AI** (`Deployment Status`, per
`sf-sheet-columns.mdc`) on each deployed object's sheet tab: check every field row
LIVE against the org (Tooling `CustomField` for custom fields — FLS-independent)
and write `Deployed` / `Not Deployed` / `Standard`. This is a gated sheet write
(`sheet-write-confirmation.mdc`) — preview the diff and confirm before applying.
Use `scripts/write_deploy_status.py`. Run it for every deployed object, every time.

## GATE 1 — FLS: ALWAYS AUTO-GRANT (mandatory, never ask)

Newly deployed custom fields have **NO field-level security** on any permission
set or profile, so a user opening the record **cannot see them** until FLS is
granted. Granting FLS is therefore **MANDATORY and AUTOMATIC** — do NOT ask the
user whether to grant it or which permission set to use. Every deployed field
gets FLS on the **default permission set `SalesFrontAdmin`**, every time.

For every deploy that created/updated fields, after verification you MUST,
WITHOUT asking:

1. State plainly which fields were just deployed and that you are auto-granting
   their FLS on `SalesFrontAdmin` (list the field count per object).
2. **Auto-grant FLS on `SalesFrontAdmin`** (the fixed default target) using the
   **LIVE-DRIVEN helper — never the delta/local-file set:**
   ```
   python scripts/grant_fls.py --object <Obj>__c --org <ORG> --apply
   # or whole-project reconciliation / backfill:
   python scripts/grant_fls.py --all-tifnt --org <ORG> --apply
   ```
   `grant_fls.py` reads the object's FULL current custom-field set from the org
   via `readMetadata(CustomObject)` (FLS-independent), classifies each field
   (formula / Summary / AutoNumber → read-only `Read=true,Edit=false`; all others
   `Read=true,Edit=true`; required-flagged + Master-Detail → SKIP, implicitly
   visible and cannot carry `fieldPermissions`), diffs against the permission
   set's existing `FieldPermissions`, and inserts ONLY the missing ones
   (idempotent + non-destructive — an existing/manually-set read-only field is
   left untouched). This MUST drive off the live field set, NOT the current
   deploy's local files: a local/delta-scoped grant silently leaves fields from
   earlier batches unpermissioned forever (see the 2026-09-11 KB lesson — 106
   fields were missing FLS because the old grant only looked at the delta).
3. The helper **live re-verifies** after inserting; a field deploy is NOT "done"
   while any deployable (non-required, non-MD) custom field is still missing from
   the permission set.

The permission-set deploy writes to the org.If the user has explicitly named a DIFFERENT permission
set for this deploy, honor that instead of `SalesFrontAdmin`; otherwise always
default to `SalesFrontAdmin` silently.

You may NOT close out a field deploy as "done" without having auto-granted Gate 1
FLS on `SalesFrontAdmin` (or the user's explicitly named alternative).

### GATE 1b — FLS MUST be re-checked on ANY visibility-affecting metadata change

Gate 1 is NOT limited to brand-new fields. It fires for **every deploy that
updates existing fields**, and it MUST be re-evaluated whenever a field's
metadata changes in a way that alters its **implicit** visibility, because such a
change can silently make a field invisible:

- **`required=true → false` (or → blank):** a required field is IMPLICITLY visible
  and CANNOT carry `fieldPermissions`; the FLS grant correctly skips it. The moment
  it becomes optional it LOSES that implicit visibility and has NO explicit FLS →
  it is invisible. You MUST run Gate 1 for every field whose requiredness was
  flipped off in this deploy (grant Read+Edit, or read-only for
  formula/Summary/AutoNumber).
- **Master-Detail → Lookup conversion:** the field was implicitly visible as MD;
  as a Lookup it needs explicit FLS. Re-check.
- Any other change that removes implicit visibility.

Conversely, a field flipped `required=false → true`, or converted Lookup→MD, no
longer needs (and cannot keep) explicit FLS — that is expected, not a gap.

Practical rule: after redeploying existing fields, do NOT assume "already
deployed ⇒ FLS already handled". Diff each object's LIVE FLS set (FLS-independent
Tooling `CustomField` for the full field list, `FieldPermissions` for what the
permission set already grants — NEVER `FieldDefinition`/`sobject describe`, which
are FLS-gated and under-report). Any deployable, non-required, non-Master-Detail
custom field that is missing from the permission set is an FLS GAP → surface it
and run Gate 1. This is exactly how the `IncidentalExpenses` /
`StandaloneIncidentalExpenses` / `IncidentalExpensesDetail` post-flip gap (91
fields) and the `DealDetail` optional-field gap (23 fields) were missed — see the
2026-08-28 KB lesson.

## GATE 1c — OBJECT-LEVEL PERMISSIONS: ALWAYS AUTO-GRANT (mandatory, never ask)

Field-Level Security (GATE 1) is NOT enough. `FieldPermissions` only control which
FIELDS a user may see/edit — they do NOT grant access to the OBJECT itself. Without
an **`ObjectPermissions`** row on the permission set, the user cannot open, list,
view, or create the record AT ALL; the tab throws "insufficient privileges" and the
granted field FLS is irrelevant because the record is unreachable. Therefore, for
EVERY object whose fields were just deployed (new object AND field-only deploys to
an existing object), granting object-level CRUD on the default permission set
**`SalesFrontAdmin`** is **MANDATORY and AUTOMATIC** — exactly like GATE 1. Do NOT
ask whether to grant it or which permission set to use.

For every deployed object, after verification you MUST, WITHOUT asking:

1. State plainly that you are auto-granting object permissions on `SalesFrontAdmin`.
2. **Auto-grant `ObjectPermissions`** = Read + Create + Edit + Delete (full CRUD)
   for each deployed object on `SalesFrontAdmin`, inserting only the MISSING rows
   (idempotent). Use the helper:
   ```
   python scripts/grant_object_perms.py --objects <Obj>__c[,<Obj2>__c,...] --org <ORG> --apply
   # or, to close the whole-project gap:
   python scripts/grant_object_perms.py --all-tifnt --org <ORG> --apply
   ```
3. **Master-Detail dependency:** a junction/detail object's `ObjectPermissions`
   Read DEPENDS on Read of its Master parent (`FIELD_INTEGRITY_EXCEPTION:
   Permission Read <child> depends on permission(s): Read <parent>`). Grant Read on
   the parent FIRST (even a cross-namespace `TI_Logi_*` parent — minimal Read is
   enough), then the child. `grant_object_perms.py` surfaces this; resolve the
   parent then retry the child.
4. **Verify object perms LIVE** afterwards (query `ObjectPermissions` on the
   permission set) — never trust the insert response alone. A deploy is NOT "done"
   while any deployed object shows `ObjectPermissions = NONE` on `SalesFrontAdmin`.

Honor a user-named alternative permission set if one was explicitly given;
otherwise always default to `SalesFrontAdmin` silently. Per
`deploy-no-password-policy.mdc` no password is required, but the grant is still
verified live. This is exactly how ALL 31 deployed `TI_Fnt_` objects ended up with
`ObjectPermissions = NONE` (users could see fields but could not open any object) —
see the 2026-09-09 KB lesson.

## GATE 2 — FlexiPage: MANDATORY check, conditional create

Whether a Lightning Record Page (FlexiPage) is built depends on whether the
object's sheet carries page-layout placement info. The CHECK is mandatory; the
BUILD is conditional on that info existing.

Read LIVE from the object's sheet tab (per `read-sheet-live.mdc`) and locate, by
header name (per `sf-sheet-columns.mdc`):
- **Tab** = column **Z** (top-level record-page tab placement), and
- **section** = column **AA** (section within a tab).

Then, for each deployed object:

1. **If the object has Tab and/or section info** (any non-blank Z/AA values on its
   deployable, non-WIP field rows) → you MUST build and deploy a FlexiPage from
   that info:
   - section-only (AA populated, Z blank) → flat sectioned page via
     `gen_record_page.py`.
   - Tab + section (both Z and AA populated) → tabbed page via
     `gen_tabbed_page.py` (top-level tabs from Z, sections from AA).
   - Only place fields that actually EXIST in the org (filter against a live
     field set), respect the 100-field-per-column limit (multi-column split),
     confirm the FlexiPage API name with the user, and deploy. Then you MUST
     set it as the object's org-default record page per **GATE 2b** below (this
     is NOT optional and is NOT done by deploying the FlexiPage alone).Verify the
     page live.
2. **If the object has NO Tab/section info** (Z and AA blank across all its field
   rows) → SKIP FlexiPage creation for that object, and say so explicitly (the
   object keeps the system default record page). Skipping is only allowed when
   the info is genuinely absent — never skip an object that has section data.

Report per object which branch was taken (built `<PageName>` vs skipped — no
Tab/section info).

## GATE 2b — MANDATORY: assign the FlexiPage as the object's ORG DEFAULT

Deploying a `FlexiPage` only makes it AVAILABLE — it does NOT activate it. A newly
deployed record page is inert until it is assigned. Whenever GATE 2 built and
deployed a FlexiPage for an object, you MUST also make it the object's **org
default** record page in the SAME flow (never leave it on the system default, and
never rely on a manual App-Builder "Activation" click — that is exactly how the
`RecevingAndQuoteAndWorkRelation` page shipped unassigned).

The org-default assignment is NOT stored on the FlexiPage. It is an
`actionOverrides` block nested inside the **CustomObject** metadata (per the
Metadata API `actionOverride` docs). Add BOTH form factors (desktop + phone) to
`objects/<Obj>__c/<Obj>__c.object-meta.xml`:

```xml
<actionOverrides>
    <actionName>View</actionName>
    <comment>Action override created by Lightning App Builder during activation.</comment>
    <content><FlexiPageApiName></content>
    <formFactor>Large</formFactor>
    <skipRecordTypeSelect>false</skipRecordTypeSelect>
    <type>Flexipage</type>
</actionOverrides>
<actionOverrides>
    <actionName>View</actionName>
    <comment>Action override created by Lightning App Builder during activation.</comment>
    <content><FlexiPageApiName></content>
    <formFactor>Small</formFactor>
    <skipRecordTypeSelect>false</skipRecordTypeSelect>
    <type>Flexipage</type>
</actionOverrides>
```

Then deploy the `CustomObject` (object-meta only — fields are separate components
and are NOT touched). This CustomObject deploy is a separate org write from the
FlexiPage deploy. Afterwards VERIFY live that the override
landed (e.g. read the object's `actionOverrides` back via `readMetadata`
CustomObject, or confirm in the org) before reporting GATE 2 complete.

Skip GATE 2b only for objects where GATE 2 was itself skipped (no Tab/section
info → no FlexiPage built).

## Guardrails

- Both gates run for EVERY object in the deploy set, every time — single-object
  and multi-object deploys alike. Do not batch-skip.
- Gate 1 (AUTO-GRANT FLS on `SalesFrontAdmin`) is mandatory and automatic — never
  ask whether to grant or which permission set;Honor a user-named alternative perm set
  if one was explicitly given; otherwise default to `SalesFrontAdmin` silently.
- Gate 1c (AUTO-GRANT `ObjectPermissions` CRUD on `SalesFrontAdmin`) is mandatory
  and automatic for every deployed object — never ask. Field FLS without object
  perms leaves the object unreachable. Grant the Master-Detail parent's Read first
  when a junction/detail child depends on it. Verify object perms LIVE; a deployed
  object with `ObjectPermissions = NONE` is a MISS.
- Gate 2's CHECK is mandatory; the BUILD is mandatory when Tab/section info
  exists and skipped only when it is truly absent.
- GATE 2b (org-default `actionOverrides` on the CustomObject) is MANDATORY
  whenever GATE 2 built a FlexiPage — a deployed-but-unassigned page is a MISS.
  Never defer it to a manual App-Builder click.
- All sheet reads for Tab/section are LIVE; never evaluate them from a cached
  snapshot.
- FLS uses the Tooling-API / metadata view of fields (FLS-independent), never
  `sobject describe`, to enumerate what to grant.
